      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182820/assets/83500660f574c604d0cd1bab3849bf252de7d8e98bd7da2bb163c6178ad7383a.png)\n\nIf the side that is opposite to a ${45}^{∘}$ angle in an isosceles right triangle has a length of $\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{2}},$ what is the length of the hypotenuse?",
      "worked_solution": "The hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are the sides that are opposite the ${45}^{∘}$ angles. So in the given triangle, the legs have length $\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{2}}.$\n\nConsequently, the hypotenuse has a length of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=12.\n$$",
